$Module gitcache 3 "Git smart-HTTP request classification and canonical cache keys"
$ABI strict

DESCRIPTION
===========

Classifies git smart-HTTP requests (as sent to github.com and compatible
servers) and builds stable cache keys for the POST requests git uses for
fetch and clone. Two clients asking for the same objects get the same key even
if they differ in client telemetry (``agent=``, ``session-id=``) or in the
order of set-like lines (``want``, ``ref-prefix``).

Example::

	import gitcache;

	sub vcl_recv {
		if (gitcache.kind() != "") {
			if (gitcache.kind() == "receive-pack") { return (pipe); }
			set req.http.X-Cache-Key = gitcache.cache_key();
			if (req.http.X-Cache-Key == "") { return (pass); }
		}
	}

	sub vcl_hash {
		hash_data(req.http.X-Cache-Key);
		return (lookup);
	}

$Function STRING kind()

Restricted to the client side.

Returns what the request is, derived from method, URL and, for upload-pack
POSTs, the buffered body:

* ``info-refs``: ``GET .../info/refs?service=git-upload-pack``
* ``info-refs-push``: ``GET .../info/refs?service=git-receive-pack``
* ``ls-refs``, ``object-info``, ``bundle-uri``: protocol v2 commands
* ``fetch``: any other v2 command, normally ``command=fetch``
* ``upload-pack``: protocol v0/v1 ``POST .../git-upload-pack``
* ``probe``: the bare ``0000`` POST git sends before a large request
* ``receive-pack``: ``POST .../git-receive-pack`` (push, never cache)
* ``lfs-batch``: ``POST .../info/lfs/objects/batch``
* ``lfs-object``: ``GET`` of an LFS object: ``.../info/lfs/objects/<sha256>``,
  or any ``GET`` whose last path segment is a 64-hex SHA-256 (a transfer URL
  chosen by the server, e.g. ``/repository/<repo>/<oid>``)
* ``lfs-other``: any other LFS endpoint (locks, upload ``verify``, ...); never cache
* ``dumb-refs``: dumb-protocol mutable files (``info/refs`` without
  ``service``, ``HEAD``, ``objects/info/packs``, alternates)
* ``dumb-object``: dumb-protocol immutable objects and packs
* empty string: not a git request

For a POST to upload-pack the body is buffered with ``std.cache_req_body()``
semantics (limit 10MB) if it is not already.

$Function STRING repo()

Restricted to the client side.

The lowercased ``owner/name`` of the repository, without leading slash or
``.git``. Empty if the request is not a git request.

$Function INT protocol()

Restricted to the client side.

The wire protocol version from the ``Git-Protocol`` header: 0, 1 or 2.

$Function STRING body_hash(BYTES limit = 10485760)

Restricted to the client side.

Hex SHA-256 of the canonical form of the upload-pack request body. Buffers
the body if necessary. Returns an empty string when the body is larger than
``limit``, is not valid pkt-line framing or on failure; the request must then
not be cached by body.

$Function STRING credential()

Restricted to the client side.

A short, non-reversible fingerprint (truncated SHA-256) of the
``Authorization`` header, or an empty string if the request carries none.
Never log the header itself; this value is safe to use in keys and logs.

$Function STRING cache_key(BYTES limit = 10485760, STRING scope = "", BOOL share_credentials = 0, BOOL lfs_batch = 0)

Restricted to the client side.

A complete cache key such as
``<scope>/torvalds/linux.git/git-upload-pack/v2/<sha256>``. Empty for
anything that must not be cached: push traffic, non-git requests, oversize or
malformed bodies.

``scope`` is prepended verbatim. Use it to separate upstreams that share
``owner/name`` space, for example the Host of each GitHub Enterprise Server
instance fronted by one cache.

``lfs_batch`` enables caching of Git LFS batch responses, download operations
only. Those responses contain time-limited transfer URLs, so the TTL must be
shorter than their expiry; upload batches are never cached.

Requests carrying an ``Authorization`` header get a key that includes
``credential()``, so a private repository's data is never served to a client
that did not present the same credentials. Set ``share_credentials`` only
when access is verified separately (for example by a preflight to
``info/refs``) before a cached object is delivered.

$Object cache(STRING scope = "", DURATION refs_ttl = 5, DURATION pack_ttl = 604800,
    DURATION lfs_batch_ttl = 10, BOOL preflight = 1, BOOL lfs_batch = 0,
    BOOL debug = 0, STRING purge_token = "", BYTES limit = 10485760,
    DURATION transfer_timeout = 3600)

A complete caching policy for git over HTTP. With it, the VCL is one line per
subroutine::

	import gitcache;

	sub vcl_init {
		new git = gitcache.cache(scope = "ghes1");
	}
	sub vcl_recv             { if (git.recv()) { return (hash); } }
	sub vcl_hash             { if (git.hash()) { return (lookup); } }
	sub vcl_backend_fetch    { git.backend_fetch(); }
	sub vcl_backend_response { if (git.backend_response()) { return (deliver); } }
	sub vcl_deliver          { if (git.deliver()) { return (restart); } }

Requests that are not cacheable git traffic (including every push) fall
through to the rest of the VCL untouched.

``scope`` separates upstreams, see ``cache_key()``. ``refs_ttl`` applies to
refs advertisements and listings, which change as people push; they are kept
for a short time with no grace period, so a stale tip is never served while
revalidating. ``pack_ttl`` applies to packs and content-addressed objects, which are fixed
by the request and never change, so it can be long (one week by default);
because history can be rewritten or deleted at the origin, purge a repository
after such a change.
``lfs_batch_ttl`` applies to LFS batch responses when ``lfs_batch`` is true.
``transfer_timeout`` is how long one pack or LFS object transfer may take,
both waiting for the origin's first byte and sending to the client; Varnish's
defaults (60 s and 600 s) cut off a cold clone of a large repository.

``preflight`` shares packs between users and re-checks access first: before a
cached pack is delivered, the same user's credential-scoped ``info/refs`` is
fetched (or served from cache for ``refs_ttl``) and the pack is only delivered
if the origin accepted it. A refusal from the origin is passed to the client
unchanged. With ``preflight`` off, packs are cached per credential instead.

``debug`` adds ``X-GC-Kind`` and ``X-GC-Hits`` response headers.

``purge_token`` enables ``.purge()``; leave it empty to disable purging.

``limit`` bounds the size of request bodies that are buffered and hashed.

$Method BOOL .recv()
$Restrict vcl_recv

Classify the request and decide whether it is cacheable git traffic. Returns
true if so, and the VCL should ``return (hash)``. Removes the module's
internal headers from client requests, and for packs sets up the access
check described above.

$Method BOOL .hash()
$Restrict vcl_hash

Adds the cache key to the hash. Returns true if it did, and the VCL should
``return (lookup)``; false for requests that are not git traffic, which keep
the built-in hash.

$Method VOID .backend_fetch()
$Restrict vcl_backend_fetch

Adjusts the backend request: restores POST for POST-based requests, and turns
the access check into a bodyless GET.

$Method BOOL .backend_response()
$Restrict vcl_backend_response

Applies the TTL and grace policy, refuses to cache anything but a 200, and
tags the object with its repository so ``.purge()`` can find it. Returns
true for git traffic, and the VCL should ``return (deliver)``; this skips the
built-in handling, which would honour the origin's ``Cache-Control: no-cache``
(git servers send it on these responses).

$Method BOOL .deliver()
$Restrict vcl_deliver

Returns true if the access check succeeded and the VCL should
``return (restart)`` to serve the pack; false otherwise, including when the
origin refused the check, in which case its answer is delivered unchanged.

$Method INT .purge()
$Restrict vcl_recv

Handles ``POST <path>?repo=owner/name`` with an ``X-Purge-Token`` header:
removes every cached object of that repository, for use from a push webhook.
Returns the HTTP status for the VCL to answer with: 200, 400 (bad repository
name), 403 (missing or wrong token, or purging disabled), 405 (not a POST) or
500. Use as ``return (synth(git.purge()));`` on the path of your choice.
